  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe threat of heavy rainfall and potential for flash flooding\ncontinues across much of the area, north and west of a Cross \nPlains to Sonora line Thursday night through Friday morning. A\nFlood Watch is in place for Thursday afternoon through Friday\nmorning for the increasing potential for heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding. WPC currently has much of the area outlooked in a\nMarginal to Slight Risk for Excessive rainfall, with a small area\nof Moderate Risk across parts of the Northern Edwards Plateau. For\nmoisture, PW values will increase to 1.5 to 2.3 inches, low level\nsoutherly flow will be advecting copious amounts of low level \nmoisture across the western part of the area, and some energy from\nremnants of Hurricane Simon will be transported into this system \ntoo. In addition, about a 6 hour window of strong overrunning \nbehind a cold front mainly across the southern Concho Valley and \nCrockett County for early Friday morning. Please keep up with \nthe latest weather information and have multiple ways of\nreceiving weather alerts and information. \n\nWe begin to dry out this weekend, as this system weakens and \nshears out, and another storm system will bring a chance of rain \nfor the middle of next week. As for temperatures, temperatures\nwill start to cool Thursday night, with high temperatures Friday \nthrough Saturday remaining in the 60s, then gradually warming into\nthe mid 70s to low 80s.",
